title: Log storage
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: How the log is stored — one form of entry for one or many metarecords, its targets and its before in chunks, the indexes beside it — and how one write lays it down.

The log lives in the repository's store, beside the data it describes — one transaction
writes both ([[Store tables]]). (Watcher events are not here: they are buffered in
memory.) The log is a sequence: operations are numbered in the order they were
written, and none names a parent ([[The operation log]],
[[Why the log stops being a tree]]). HEAD — the newest operation, the change feed's
cursor — is `log_head` in `global_primary_state`.

!! One form of entry

Every entry of the log is ''one operation on a list of metarecords'', its
''targets''; an operation on one metarecord is a list of one. An entry takes as many
operation ids as it has targets: the operation on the `k`-th target is `first + k`,
and what reads the log is given, for that id, the operation a write to that target
alone would have logged — a revert, the history, the change feed and the trash
correlation know nothing of entries. Ids are never reused. The log is ''counted by
entry'', one key each, which is what it costs.

```
log_entries  first op id → log context · timestamp · origin · effect · file action
                           · [first reverted op] · parameters · target count
log_targets  first op id · chunk number → { uuid } · pending ranks · before of the chunk
```

A chunk holds a fixed number of targets (4096): an operation on one metarecord is two
keys, with no case of its own. The targets are ''uuids'', never dense ids: one form,
nothing for a reindex to rewrite. A write to many records, which holds its records as
a bitmap of dense ids, turns them into uuids as it writes (`uuids`, one key per id, in
the order of the ids).

|!Header part |!Holds |
|log context |the innermost context of the write ([[Log contexts]]) |
|timestamp |when it was written (Unix ms): one write's entries share it |
|origin |one byte: client, watcher, trash ([[Operations and their origin]]) — not read off the context: a command can mix ordinary writes and trashing |
|effect |one byte, below |
|file action |one byte: none, `file_deleted`, `file_moved`, `file_modified` — what an undo does to the files ([[Filesystem coordination]]) |
|first reverted op |for an entry a revert wrote: the operation `first + k` reverts `reverted + k`, the reverted ids being consecutive, in the order of the targets |
|parameters |those of the effect, below; values in the compact form ([[Value encoding in the store]]) |

The wire still names an operation by its type ([[Operations and their origin]]): the
file action when there is one, else the effect's (`create_metarecord`,
`delete_metarecord`, `set_metarecord`, `append_field`, `delete_field`, `set_field`).

!! Effects, and the before

An operation keeps what it found, its ''before'', and nothing of the state it left: a
revert applies its inverse to the current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]).
The before of a chunk is grouped by value: `{ field · type · value · the ranks, in the
chunk, of the targets that held it }`.

|!Effect |!Parameters |!Before |
|create |— |— |
|delete |— |every row of each target |
|set_record |every row |every row of each target |
|add |field · value |the targets that held `nothing` in the field (an add takes it away, [[Nothing versus unknown]]) |
|remove |field · value |— (undone by an add) |
|set |field · values (one or more) |the field's previous values |
|unset |field |the field's previous values |

The before of a ''deletion of many records'' is not written when the deletion is: it
is in the records, which its pending `delete` keeps as they were. The ranks of those
targets are the chunk's ''pending ranks'' (a roaring bitmap, a few bytes for a whole
chunk), and the log reads their before in the record. The fold copies it into the
chunks, chunk by chunk, without rewriting the header or another chunk; so does a write
to a record an undo of the deletion gave back, for that record alone
([[Mass writes in the store]]). Any other entry has no pending rank.

A write to one metarecord gives its effect from what it did: an append is an `add`; a
removal of one value a `remove`; a removal of every row of a field (`field remove`
without a value) an `unset`, which keeps the rows as its before; a set that writes no
value an `unset` too.

A directory's `file_deleted` gives each descendant its own operation in the same write.

!! Compaction

An operation that differs from the last entry of the log only by its targets is added
to it — its last chunk rewritten, or a new chunk — when that entry is the last of the
log, of the same context, and the targets are disjoint. A revert's entry is extended
only by the revert of the next operation (first reverted = last reverted + 1). So a
loop of `mf … field add` over a thousand files, inside one context, is one entry.

A deletion is not extended: the undo of a deletion of many records knows it by its
entry. Whether the entry already names the target is asked of its targets, which the
store keeps in memory between the writes that extend it (read from the chunks once,
and again after a write that failed). An operation added to an entry takes its
timestamp.

Within one write transaction nothing else writes, so the chunk an entry is extended in
is held decoded by the transaction (`KvTxn::log_chunk`): each operation pushes its
target and its before to it, and it is encoded and put once — at the commit, when the
entry moves to its next chunk, or when another entry is appended. Every read of the
log inside the transaction sees it (`Read`'s `log_chunk` hook, under `Read::chunk`);
what writes the chunks otherwise (`write_befores`, `remove_entry`) writes it out
first. Before, each operation decoded and encoded the whole chunk — up to 4096 targets
and their before —, quadratic within a chunk: a watcher batch of 4096 events spent
over 90 % of its write there (5.2 s of which 4.9 s). `KvStore::log_chunk_codings`
counts both (`tests/kv_cost.rs`: 5000 operations extending one entry encode its two
chunks once each, and decode none).

The header (its count of targets) and its key in `log_entries_by_context` are still
written by each operation: their cost does not grow with the entry, and a walk of
`log_entries` inside the transaction then reads them as they are.

Compaction happens at the append only, where nothing is to be rewritten. Compacting
entries afterwards stays possible: every key of the absorbed entry (`log_targets`,
`log_entries_by_context`, `log_reverted_by`) would then be rewritten under the id of the
one that stays.

!! Beside the entries

|!Table |!Key |!Kept |
|`log_entries_by_context` |context · first op id → target count |at the append; a compaction updates the count. A context's operations, their number, its first and last |
|`log_reverted_by` |reverted entry's first op id · first reverted · last reverted · the op that reverts the first |at the append, one key per pair of entries; a revert of a whole context writes one per entry it covers |
|`log_watcher_entries`, `log_user_entries` |first op id |at the append: the two retention classes ([[Automatic retention]]) |
|`log_entries_by_metarecord` |uuid · op id |at the idle fold ([[Folding in idle time]]), one key per target, up to `log_entries_by_metarecord_indexed_up_to` in `global_derived_state`; a step indexes about 64 Ki targets |

Whether `X` is reverted: the entry holding `X` is known; its keys in
`log_reverted_by` are read, and if first ≤ `X` ≤ last, `X` is reverted by the op that
reverts the first, plus `X − first reverted`. A revert's entry that grows replaces its
key; a prune of the revert removes its keys, and a prune of the reverted entry those
under it.

A metarecord's history after an id reads `log_entries_by_metarecord`, then the entries
not yet indexed, searching their targets (in any order: a compaction appends to them).
Its cost is loading those entries; the idle fold keeps them few. A reindex touches
none of the log: it holds no dense id.

The first reverted op is read by displays and by the undo selection — which is why a
revert records it rather than merely looking like one. Nothing on the write path
branches on it, and it may dangle: pruning and retention may remove the operation it
names.

!! Versions recomputed

The version is never restored from the log: it is a hash of the content
([[Metarecord version]]), so the state a revert writes has the version it should. An
entry records no version, nor what it added to one. The one reader of a past version —
a revert matching a trash entry on the version a file had before the watcher's flush
([[Filesystem coordination]]) — rebuilds the metarecord's state before the context by
undoing its operations since, newest first, from the current state, as a revert would,
and computes the version of that state. It costs the operations on that one
metarecord.

!! One write

Every request that changes metadata goes through one `log::Writer`, in one transaction:
its context is checked ([[Log contexts]]); for each change, the affected rows
are read (the before), the change is applied, and the operation appended — to the last
entry when it can be; finally HEAD is recorded and the transaction commits. Every
writer — reconcile included — follows it, which is what keeps the data and HEAD
consistent ([[Writes and atomicity]]).
